@* Introduction.
This document is the complete program \emph{Texture Explorer}, a small interactive
tool for a course in computer graphics. It shows a material---a set of images that
together describe a surface---and lets the student look at every image of the set,
apply the material to a simple solid, move the camera and the light, and, above all,
\emph{ask questions}: pointing with the mouse at any texel of any map, the program
answers with the texture coordinates of that texel, the values stored there in every
map, and the normal vector that the bump map produces at that point, both in the
tangent space of the surface and in the coordinate system of the world.

The program is written in the style that Donald E. Knuth called \emph{literate
programming}: instead of writing a program for a computer and then documenting it, we
write an essay for human beings in which the program appears, piece by piece, in the
order in which it is easiest to understand. The pieces are later rearranged
mechanically into the files that a compiler wants. The tool that does this,
\texttt{lp.ps1}, is described in the file \texttt{README.md} next to this web.

@ The main ideas a student should take away are few, and they reappear constantly:

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Texture space.} Every point of a surface carries coordinates $(u,v)$ in the
unit square; the images of the material are indexed by them. How a shape is mapped onto
the square is a design decision, and different shapes show different trade-offs.
\item \emph{Height fields.} A grey image $h(u,v)$ can be used in two very different ways:
as a \emph{bump map}, which only tilts the normal used for shading, or as a
\emph{displacement map}, which really moves the surface.
\item \emph{Tangent space.} The normal computed from a bump map is expressed relative to
the surface, in the frame $(\vect T,\vect B,\vect N)$; it becomes a vector of the world
only after multiplication by that frame.
\item \emph{Roughness.} A roughness map changes, point by point, how sharp or blurry the
specular highlight is, through a microfacet model of reflection.
\end{itemize}

@ The program consists of the following files, each of which is a module of this web.

\medskip
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\texttt{src/Mesh.h}, \texttt{src/Mesh.cpp} & parametric shapes and their texture mapping \\
\texttt{src/Material.h}, \texttt{src/Material.cpp} & loading the maps of a material \\
\texttt{src/Inspector.h}, \texttt{src/Inspector.cpp} & the probe: values and normals at a texel \\
\texttt{shaders/pbr.hlsl} & displacement, bump mapping and lighting on the GPU \\
\texttt{shaders/gizmo.hlsl} & coloured lines: arrows and the light marker \\
\texttt{src/Renderer.h}, \texttt{src/Renderer.cpp} & Direct3D 11 device, targets and drawing \\
\texttt{src/App.h}, \texttt{src/App.cpp} & the user interface \\
\texttt{src/main.cpp} & the window and the main loop \\
\texttt{CMakeLists.txt} & the build description \\
\end{tabular}
\medskip

We shall meet them roughly from the bottom up: first the geometry, because texture
coordinates are a property of geometry; then the images; then the mathematics of the
probe; then the GPU code that does the same mathematics for every pixel; and finally
the machinery around them. The images themselves come from two libraries of free
(CC0) materials, \emph{ambientCG} and \emph{Poly Haven}; a PowerShell script downloads ten
of them and writes a \emph{manifest} that describes their origin.

@ One frame of the program, seen from far away, goes like this. The user interface is
rebuilt from scratch (Dear ImGui is an ``immediate mode'' library); while it is being
built we learn whether the mouse is over one of the images, and where. If it is, the
\emph{inspector} computes everything that can be said about that texel. Then the 3D scene
is drawn into an off-screen image, together with arrows that show the inspector's
vectors at the corresponding point of the solid, and the off-screen image is shown in
the ``Viewport'' panel. Finally the whole user interface is drawn on the screen.

@ \label{conventions}\emph{Conventions.} Throughout the program the world is right-handed with the $y$ axis
pointing up, and the camera and the light orbit around the origin, where the solid
sits. Texture space follows the convention of Direct3D: $(0,0)$ is the \emph{top-left}
corner of an image, $u$ grows to the right and $v$ grows \emph{downwards}.

This has a consequence that is worth stating once and for all. Let $\vect P(u,v)$ be a
point of a surface, $\vect T=\partial\vect P/\partial u$ and $\vect B=\partial\vect P/\partial v$
its tangent and bitangent, and $\vect N$ the outward normal. An observer outside the
surface sees $\vect T$ pointing to the right and, because $v$ grows downwards in the image,
$\vect B$ pointing \emph{down}. Right $\times$ down is \emph{into} the surface in a
right-handed system, so for a correctly oriented (not mirrored) texture
\[ \vect T\times\vect B = -\vect N. \]
Every shape in the next part has been checked against this rule.

@ \label{encoding}Vectors of unit length can be stored as colours by the affine map
$\vect n\mapsto\frac12\vect n+\frac12$, which takes $[-1,1]$ to $[0,1]$. Normal maps use
it, and so do the colour swatches of the inspector and the ``normal'' views of the shader.
A normal that points straight out of the surface, $(0,0,1)$ in tangent space, becomes the
characteristic lavender $(0.5,0.5,1)$ of normal maps.
